\begin_layout Subsubsection
Inner loop
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A class of the Inner loop type will create an output file.
 Let us start by explaining, in detail, one example: the loop::ped_per_loci
 class.
 There is only one 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

real
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 method, 
\emph on
run_loop
\emph default
(), in the class, but quite a few virtual function that are stubbed out
 as empty to be replaced in a derived class as necessary.
 The goal of this class is to open and output file and then iterate over
 all the pedigrees and then in each pedigree iterate over each person and
 then for each person iterate over all the markers in some chromosome.
 The file is open on line 8.
 Line 11 starts the loop on pedigrees, and line 20 starts the loop on loci
 on the specified chromosome.
 Line 11 sets and increments _ped, which is used on line 17 to define _tp,
 a pointer to the pedigree info.
 A similar arrangements happens on line 20/23 also on 27/29/30.
 Again these constructs define variables that can be accessed, but by what?
 The interesting part of the loop is lines 18/46, 25/44, and 41 which invoke
 the virtual methods 
\emph on
ped_start/ped_end
\emph default
, 
\emph on
per_start/per_end
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
inner
\emph default
, respectively.
 The ped_per_loci class has these functions do nothing, but a derived class
 can override the methods as needed.
 For example, if you needed to relabel the pedigree (and or persons), that
 code could be placed in the 
\emph on
ped_start
\emph default
 method.
 If you needed to print the person id, this could be done in the 
\emph on
per_star
\emph default
t method.
 This class could be used to list the genotypes for each person.
 In this case, the 
\emph on
inner
\emph default
 method would print the genotypes.
 It could also print a new line or the new line could be deferred to the
 
\emph on
per_end
\emph default
 method, thus producing a long line starting with the person id followed
 by all the allele labels.
 (Note: if all this is clear, you should see that the first option will
 only produce the person id on the first line per pedigree/person.
\end_layout

