\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One final note: it seems that there might be more Outer loop (and Inner
 loop) classes than are necessary.
 The answer is that the flexibility is necessary.
 For example, you might use the Outer loop over chromosome and trait class
 to produce a file that contains the person id and all his/her phenotypes.
 That would create a file in each trait directory (possibly labeled by the
 chromosome.) But all these files would contain the same data.
 In this case, using the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

not a loop
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 case is what is probably wanted.
 Of course, if the analysis needs a control file for each trait and chromosome,
 then the first choice was correct.
 And if the control file has some lines that depend on the trait/chromosome
 and others that the user would set depending on the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

subanalysis
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, you might use both loops and when the control file with the trait/chromosome
 information is written add a line to include the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

subanalysis
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 control file.
\end_layout

